WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:05.160
Hey guys, it's Jan here. If you've watched
my review and my 25 fun facts video, you'll

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.820
know I really enjoyed Disney's latest animated
movie, Zootropolis.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:13.050
So, I wanted to look back over the five funniest
moments from the movie.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.449
Obviously, spoilers ahead if you haven't seen
the film already!

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:20.830
And I've got a great giveaway to win this
beautiful Art of Zootropolis hardback book.

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:25.070
It'll be on my Zootropolis easter egg video,
so make sure you're subscribed and I'll add

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.340
a link here to the video as well when it's
ready.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:31.789
Kicking off at number 5 is Nick and Judy's
encounter with Yax the hippie yak and his

00:00:31.789 --> 00:00:35.109
au naturel furry friends at the Mystic Spring
Oasis.

00:00:35.109 --> 00:00:39.800
I like the fact that although Yax seems to
be a bit out of it, he's actually the one

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:45.030
who remembers every detail of Mr Otterton's
last visit while Nangi, the yoga-practising

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:48.710
elephant, has no recollection whatsoever of
the missing otter.

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:53.429
All of which, of course, turns on its head
the saying that 'elephants never forget.'

00:00:53.429 --> 00:00:58.329
And what about that brilliant scene at the
DMV, aka the Department of Mammal Vehicles,

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:04.379
which is staffed solely by sloths?!
Nick's friend Flash takes literally all day

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:09.770
to look up a licence plate to help Judy.
I love the pacing of this scene, the pauses

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:16.009
Flash takes between words, his absolute insistence
on finishing each and every sentence even

00:01:16.009 --> 00:01:21.049
when the conversation's already moved on,
and the way he reacts to Nick's joke, a smile

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:26.460
spreading super-slowly across his face and
his eyes until he's actually laughing.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.700
Oh, and of course, it's funny to watch the
change in Judy's reactions as she goes from

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:37.030
positively perky at the start to full-on frustrated
by the end, and I just love the way Nick sort

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:42.320
of glances at her from the side, a half-smirk
on his face, because he knows exactly how

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.210
this is going to go down.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:48.590
I absolutely adore the whole elephant ice
cream parlour scam that Nick pulls.

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:52.999
It's just such a beautifully thought-out and
animated sequence of events, with loads of

00:01:52.999 --> 00:01:56.780
great jokes throughout.
You've got Nick and his supposed elephant-loving

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:02.049
son who pull a fast one on an unwitting Judy,
getting her to pay for their jumbo popsicle.

00:02:02.049 --> 00:02:06.630
Then they melt that Jumbo pop on a rooftop,
and pour the liquid into tracks left by Finnick's

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:11.860
paws in Tundratown's snow which creates 'Pawsicles'
which they sell to a bunch of suit-wearing

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:15.980
banker Lemmings, who then throw the sticks
into a recycling bin, where Finnick collects

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:20.370
them only for Nick to sell the sticks as red
wood to a bunch of tiny rodents!

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:24.880
It's such a genius way to introduce the character
of scam artist fox Nick and have him cross

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.180
paths with hard-working cop Judy.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.700
At number 2 is the chase scene through Little
Rodentia.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:35.150
This bit of the film is full of wonderful
sight gags and cleverly draws on a mix of

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:40.320
monster movies like Godzilla and sci-fi flicks
like Attack Of The 50-Foot Woman as we see

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:44.670
Judy pursuing the thief Duke Weaselton through
this tiny part of town.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:50.390
It's so funny when Weaselton has a tiny rodent
car on each foot and uses them to skate away

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:55.090
from Judy, and when he has to force himself
into all sorts of crazy shapes as he tries

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:59.220
to make his getaway on top of a speeding train.
And what about when all the little rodents

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:03.540
are running screaming from Judy and she politely
apologises to them as she runs by.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:08.110
And wasn't it cool when she saves Mr Big's
daughter from death by giant donut?!

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:13.000
And speaking of Mr Big, at number one is the
genius scene where Judy and Nick meet the

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:18.670
teeny tiny mob boss.
I just love that scene in all its Godfather-inspired

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:24.910
glory, from the moment his polar bear bodyguard
opens his hands to reveal that Mr Big is actually

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:31.380
a tuxedo-wearing arctic shrew with the voice
of Marlon Brando, to the movie's literal interpretation

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:38.070
of icing, Michael Giacchino's brilliant Godfather-style
music, and the whole chat about how Nick sold

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:44.230
Mr Big an expensive rug made of the fur of
a skunk's butt which Mr Big then used to bury

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:47.310
his grandmama!
And I can't forget the wedding scene with

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:51.480
all the shrews dancing, and the way the camera
pulls back to reveal they're actually all

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.730
on top of a little table with the polar bears
guarding it.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:59.730
I could watch this particular bit of the movie
over and over again and still find it funny.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:05.930
Ok, what are your favourite moments from Zootropolis?
And which bits made you really laugh out loud

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:08.790
the most?
I can't wait to find out in the comments below!

